<2026-07-30 Thu> We spent several days trying increasingly elaborate
methods to deal with the condition of a comment that starts on one
page continuing onto another page before realizing there was a simple
formatting clue (<C>...</C> after the new <CC> section starts).  I
want to avoid getting led astray like this in the future.  The
responsibility lies with me: this kind of distraction is a well-known
short-coming of contemporary AIs.

*What I've done:* I edited the agenda.org file and removed many of the
todo items that were confused or misleading. (NB: the agenda.org file
is tracked in git, so we can always recover previous states.)  I also removed the
structure, so todo items are at the top level. If we actually find it
useful to categorize them, we should use org mode's built-in tags
feature.

*** Decision
[2026-07-30] Added three specific checks to the "Simplicity First"
section of =CLAUDE.md= covering research and analysis tasks (as
distinct from the existing code-focused items): source before
inference, iteration as diagnostic signal, and "first we need
to..." as a warning sign. Added a "Shared Failure Modes" section to
=.claude/collaboration-protocol.org= noting the mutual dynamic —
Cliff's appetite for theoretical elaboration and Claude Chat's
fluency in it can reinforce each other — and flagging the implicit
=CLIFF.md= as a thread to return to. =DISPOSITION: adopt=

